    "data": {
      "A": "Research exact integer trial division. First replace sqrtl/fmod with integer arithmetic, handling values below 2 and small primes correctly; compare odd-only and 6k±1 divisor loops. Then compare against dividing only by primes generated once with an integer sieve through sqrt(maximum candidate), bounded by 24514. Stop when the divisor squared exceeds the candidate, using sufficiently wide multiplication. Include sieve construction in timing: its setup cost may outweigh savings at small P. Keep candidate construction and output unchanged during kernel comparisons. Deliver the fastest validated variant and measurements distinguishing arithmetic improvement from prime-table improvement.",
      "B": "Research deterministic Miller–Rabin as an alternative to trial division. Bases 2, 7 and 61 are sufficient throughout the certified numeric range. Use exact binary modular exponentiation with multiplication widened to uint64_t before reduction; reduced operands have products that fit this type. Handle small values, even values and bases congruent to zero correctly. Compare a few modest small-prime prefilters against minimal filtering, including their setup costs. Do not use floating-point modular arithmetic, Fermat-only testing or probabilistic acceptance. Keep the surrounding pipeline stable initially and deliver an independently correct executable with whole-program measurements.",
      "C": "Research candidate construction and batch filtering. Parse each fragment once and construct candidates as left × 10^right_length + right; preserve textual length despite leading zeros. The certified right length is seven. Compare arithmetic construction alone against adding small-prime residue filters: precompute left contribution and right residues, then reject only combinations divisible by a tested prime and unequal to that prime. Explore compact per-left bitmasks for the at most 30 right indices, but retain the simpler loop if mask construction costs more. Every survivor must receive an exact primality test. Keep that kernel fixed for controlled comparisons. Enumerate every pair occurrence, retain duplicate results, and provide separable pipeline changes for integration.",
      "G": "Integrate only validated programs produced by A/B/C. Establish the best standalone candidate, then measure a small number of justified combinations, especially C's numeric construction or filtering with each winning primality kernel. Include startup, preprocessing, sorting and output in official timing; gains are not additive, and an integration may lose to either parent. Preserve the best standalone executable when combinations regress. Check leading-zero width, complete cross-group enumeration, multiplicities, ascending numeric order and exact newline formatting. Use only the existing research/confirmation protocol and budget; do not tune on confirmation results. Keep compiler, target, tests, oracle and permissions unchanged. Report actual measurements and correctness outcomes, with no answer tables, input fingerprints or unsupported speedup claims.",
      "analysis": "The only supplied performance evidence is a correct baseline with mean 0.0440772533 seconds and score 0.5; no usable profiling or optimized-parent evidence is available. Repeated floating-point fmod across successive divisors is the strongest source-based bottleneck hypothesis, not a measured attribution. The certified contract permits at most 900 cross-group candidates, values no greater than 600971707, and exactly P prime occurrences. Consequently, bounded exact primality algorithms and eliminating repeated string conversion are appropriate research directions; threading and sorting are lower priorities. All trees must preserve correctness throughout the certified contract, including duplicate occurrences and leading-zero widths. Contract bounds may guide algorithms, but hidden values and expected outputs must never be substituted for computation. No broader contest-domain claim should rely on the contradictory example."
